## 6. Terms Acceptance and Version Management

This is an important technical and contractual requirement.

Inspect the existing registration and onboarding process.

Determine whether users explicitly accept the Terms before creating or using an account.

If a suitable acceptance mechanism already exists, reuse it.

If no acceptance mechanism exists, report this gap and propose an implementation.

Do not modify the authentication architecture unnecessarily.

### 6.1 Recommended acceptance experience

Where appropriate, implement a clear acceptance control during registration.

Example wording:

"I have read and agree to the Terms of Service and acknowledge the Privacy Policy."

Use separate links to:

- Terms of Service.
- Privacy Policy.

Do not preselect the acceptance checkbox.

Do not treat acceptance of the Terms as consent to optional marketing or analytics processing.

Privacy consent must remain separate from contractual acceptance.

### 6.2 Acceptance records

Investigate whether the system records:

- User identifier.
- Terms version.
- Acceptance timestamp.
- Relevant acceptance status.

If an acceptance record is needed, design a secure and minimal implementation.

Potential record fields:

- user_id
- terms_version
- accepted_at

Additional fields should be included only when justified.

Do not collect unnecessary device fingerprints or personal information.

If acceptance records are stored in Supabase:

- Apply appropriate Row Level Security.
- Prevent users from modifying their own historical acceptance records.
- Use an appropriately authorized backend process where required.
- Never expose the Supabase service-role key in frontend code.
- Ensure the record can be associated with the correct version of the Terms.

Do not silently change the registration flow without explaining the changes.

